{
  "id": "headlights",
  "title": "Headlights",
  "arc": 1,
  "stages": [
    {
      "type": "briefing",
      "lines": [
        "Rover's headlights are LEDs. They are tiny, bright and very fussy.",
        "Give one too much and it retires early. Today you learn how to keep it happy."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "toy": {
        "kind": "led-guard",
        "prompt": "Slide the resistor up from zero. Find where the LED is happy."
      },
      "chunks": {
        "1": [
          "At zero squeeze the LED popped. With some squeeze it glowed happily. Too much squeeze and it went dark.",
          "An [[led]] is fussy: it lets almost anything through, so too much flow burns it out. A [[resistor]] squeezes the flow to a safe amount.",
          "Safe for this LED is under 30 [[milliamp]]. The resistor keeps it there."
        ],
        "2": [
          "An [[led]] has almost no resistance of its own once it is lit, so nothing limits the [[current]]. A [[resistor]] in [[series]] sets it.",
          "The LED keeps about 2 V for itself (red). The resistor takes the rest: Vs − 2 V."
        ],
        "3": [
          "An [[led]] drops a roughly fixed forward voltage Vf (about 2 V for red, more for blue). The series [[resistor]] must drop Vs − Vf at the target [[current]].",
          "Size it with R = (Vs − Vf) ÷ I, then round up to the next standard value, so the current ends up a little under target."
        ]
      },
      "help": {
        "1": "The LED is like a fire hose with no tap. The resistor is the tap.",
        "2": "Picture a water slide. Without a gate, everyone rushes down at once. The resistor is the gate.",
        "3": "Try 330 Ω with a 5 V battery: the flow is about 9 mA. Happy LED.",
        "4": "Slide the resistor until the readout says between 5 and 25 mA. That is the happy zone."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "formula": "led-resistor",
      "intro": {
        "1": "This rule tells you which resistor to pick. Into the Formula Book it goes.",
        "2": "The LED keeps Vf for itself, so the [[resistor]] only gets Vs − Vf. Then it is Ohm's law. Into the Formula Book.",
        "3": "R = (Vs − Vf) ÷ I. Into the Formula Book."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example",
      "steps": {
        "1": [
          "A 9 V battery. The LED keeps 2 V for itself.",
          "9 − 2 leaves 7 V for the resistor.",
          "We want 0.02 A of flow. R = 7 ÷ 0.02.",
          "R = 350 Ω. The shop sells 470 Ω, the next size up. A bit bigger squeeze is safe."
        ],
        "3": [
          "Vs = 9 V, Vf = 2 V, target I = 20 mA = 0.020 A.",
          "V across the resistor = 9 − 2 = 7 V.",
          "R = V ÷ I = 7 ÷ 0.020 = 350 Ω.",
          "Round up to the next E-series value, 390 or 470 Ω. Higher R means lower current, so it is safe."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "try",
      "title": "Try one",
      "questions": [
        {
          "prompt": {
            "1": "A {Vs} V battery. The LED keeps {Vf} V for itself and needs {I} A of flow. What resistor do you need, in ohms?",
            "2": "A {Vs} V battery. The LED keeps {Vf} V for itself and needs {I} A of flow. What resistor do you need, in ohms?",
            "3": "A {Vs} V battery. The LED drops {Vf} V and should carry {I} mA. What resistor, in ohms?"
          },
          "unit": "Ω",
          "gen": {
            "1": {
              "vars": {
                "Vs": {
                  "pick": [
                    6,
                    7,
                    9,
                    12
                  ]
                },
                "Vf": {
                  "pick": [
                    2
                  ]
                },
                "I": {
                  "pick": [
                    0.01,
                    0.02
                  ]
                }
              },
              "answer": "(Vs - Vf) / I"
            },
            "2": {
              "vars": {
                "Vs": {
                  "pick": [
                    5,
                    6,
                    9,
                    12
                  ]
                },
                "Vf": {
                  "pick": [
                    2,
                    2.2,
                    3
                  ]
                },
                "I": {
                  "pick": [
                    0.01,
                    0.015,
                    0.02
                  ]
                }
              },
              "answer": "(Vs - Vf) / I",
              "dp": 1
            },
            "3": {
              "vars": {
                "Vs": {
                  "pick": [
                    5,
                    6,
                    9,
                    12
                  ]
                },
                "Vf": {
                  "pick": [
                    2,
                    2.2,
                    3
                  ]
                },
                "I": {
                  "pick": [
                    5,
                    10,
                    15,
                    20
                  ]
                }
              },
              "answer": "(Vs - Vf) / (I / 1000)",
              "dp": 1
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "1": "The resistor gets {Vs} − {Vf} volts. R = (Vs − Vf) ÷ I = ({Vs} − {Vf}) ÷ {I} = {answer} Ω.",
            "2": "The resistor gets {Vs} − {Vf} volts. R = (Vs − Vf) ÷ I = ({Vs} − {Vf}) ÷ {I} = {answer} Ω.",
            "3": "V across the resistor = {Vs} − {Vf}. R = that ÷ ({I} ÷ 1000) = {answer} Ω."
          },
          "wrong": [
            {
              "expr": "Vs / I",
              "why": "That forgot the LED keeps {Vf} V. Take it off first: {Vs} − {Vf}, then divide."
            }
          ],
          "hints": [
            "Start with the push left over for the resistor: battery minus the LED's share.",
            "Left over = {Vs} − {Vf}. Now Ohm's law: R = V ÷ I.",
            "Divide ({Vs} − {Vf}) by {I}."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "help": {
        "1": "First take away what the LED keeps. Then divide by the flow you want.",
        "2": "The battery push is shared: the LED takes some, the resistor takes the rest.",
        "3": "Example: 9 V, LED 2 V, 0.02 A. Left over 7 V. 7 ÷ 0.02 = 350 Ω.",
        "4": "Watch: subtract {Vs} − {Vf}. Divide by {I}. That is your answer. Now try again."
      }
    },
